

    - two things suffice to conduct me to the theoretic ground of an ethic:
      the phenomenon of a cosmic safehold and our concept of moral law

               cosmic safehold  + ‘moral law’ →  ethic

        : re `cosmic safehold` see ~/work/safehold/ : *The presence throughout the cosmos
          of a safehold fit for securing our long-term survival.*
        : re `moral law` see `^*Pearsall.+2003.+Oxford dictionary of English`s @ sources.brec :
         ‘(in some systems of ethics) an absolute principle defining the criteria of right action
          (whether conceived as a divine ordinance or a truth of reason)’
        : re `ethic` see doi:10.1093/OED/2656971922 : *Oxford English dictionary*, ‘ethic (n.),
          sense b,’ June 2026. : ‘A system or set of moral principles’.
        - the first evidences a source of morals, the second represents its conduit into society,
          and the two together yield under analysis the conditions of moral law
          that constitute the ground of the ethic
            : re `the first (.+ a source of morals)`
              see `^*- the phenomenon of the cosmic safehold ${same}$`
    - the phenomenon of the cosmic safehold evidences a source of morals
        - [because] what explains the presence of that safehold?
        - an aetiologic survey that aims for exhaustive coverage by typological division
          turns up the following results


                table, Known, possible explanantia for the cosmic safehold
                    ┈

                  explain-  truth mode    name of explanans
                   able    of explanans

                    yes
                            necessary
                                          physical necessity

                                          supernatural design

                        ┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈
                            contingent
                                          natural cosmogonal selection

                                          multiverse ensemble

                                          mundane design

                    ┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈┈
                    no
                                          null, brute fact



            : re `an aetiologic survey.+by typological division`
              see ~/work/safehold/aetiology/ : *Aetiology of the cosmic safehold.*
        - two artificial explanantia, three natural, and one null explanans comprise the results
        - both artificial explanantia involve what tradition has long given [us]
          to be a source of morals, namely a universal or cosmic creator

            • supernatural design by God
                : re `^*• (.+ design) .+` see `^^${same}$` @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
            • mundane design by cosmic creators
